<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  REV Grant Administration - the model-driven app the process owner works in.

  WHY PHASE 1 NEEDS AN APP
  Three approved requirements have no other surface. FR-017 needs the process owner to edit
  thresholds herself; FR-018 needs her to override an automated status and have the override
  recorded against her name; FR-019 and FR-020 need a Borderline working list and a separate
  rejected list. The saved queries that already ship with this solution exist to be shown
  somewhere, and this is that somewhere. The trustee portal is a different surface entirely
  (Code App, Phase 3, ADR-003) and is not in this release.

  ROLE ASSOCIATION IS DELIBERATELY EMPTY BELOW.
  <AppModuleRoleMaps /> stays empty because app sharing is per-environment configuration, not a
  solution component (TAD section 12). provisioning/dataverse/share-apps.ps1 associates this
  app with REV Admin and REV Service Automation at post_deploy, looking the roles up by name.

  ACCESSIBILITY
  A model-driven app inherits the platform's own accessibility behaviour, so the WCAG 2.1 AA
  obligation here (TAD section 8) falls on the parts this solution authors: every custom column
  carries a meaningful display name and a description, both of which are already present in the
  Entity.xml files, and the Setting editing surface conveys nothing by colour alone - a
  threshold's value is read from the Value column as text, not from any styling.

  EVERY ELEMENT BELOW IS GROUND TRUTH, NOT A GUESS - REWRITTEN COMPLETE 2026-08-14.
  The original hand-authored file (recorded in the Dev Summary as unvalidated, exactly like
  AppModuleSiteMap.xml one folder over) failed live import with a NullReferenceException deep
  inside Microsoft.Crm.Tools.ImportExportPublish.ImportAppModulesHandler.GetParameterXPath() -
  a generic failure with no field name attached. Rather than guess again, the user built a real
  model-driven app in DEV on rev_applicant/rev_application/rev_setting through the maker portal,
  which was then added to this solution as a component (AddSolutionComponent, ComponentType 80)
  and exported + unpacked to see exactly how Dataverse serialises a working one. The differences
  from the original guess were extensive enough that almost nothing survived unchanged:

    - WRONG: <appmoduleid>. Not part of this file. Dataverse assigns it on creation, the same
      as Role, FieldSecurityProfile and sitemapid before it - a fixed GUID here was never
      going to match what gets generated, and nothing on disk needs to reference it directly
      (see the AppModuleComponents fix below for why not).
    - WRONG: <name>, <descriptions>, <url>. None exist. The display name comes ONLY through
      <LocalizedNames><LocalizedName description="..." languagecode="1033" /></LocalizedNames>,
      the same pattern AppModuleSiteMap.xml should have followed from its own first draft.
    - WRONG CASING: <uniquename>, <clienttype>, <navigationtype>, <formfactor> are all
      lowercase here; the real elements are PascalCase - <UniqueName>, <ClientType>,
      <NavigationType>, <FormFactor> - and a case-sensitive XML lookup reading the wrong case
      gets null back, exactly like EntityName/AttributeName on FieldPermission earlier.
    - WRONG VALUES (found only by comparison, not by any error message): NavigationType was 1,
      the real default is 0. FormFactor was 2, the real default is 1 (Web).
    - WRONG: <isdefault>, <isfeatured>, <iscustomizable>, and a duplicated introducedversion/
      IntroducedVersion pair. None of the first three appear in a real export; IntroducedVersion
      appears exactly once, PascalCase.
    - WRONG: <AppModuleRoles />. There is no such element. The real one is <AppModuleRoleMaps>,
      containing <Role id="{guid}" /> entries for apps that ARE shared through the solution -
      the design decision to leave this empty and share per-environment instead (see above)
      still stands, it was only the element name that was fabricated.
    - WRONG: the sitemap AppModuleComponent used id="{fabricated-guid}". The real one below
      uses schemaName="rev_grantadministration", exactly like the type="1" table references
      that were already correct - this is what actually resolves the id-reconciliation problem
      AppModuleSiteMap.xml's header flagged as still pending: there never was a live sitemapid
      to reconcile against, because the reference was never meant to be by id at all.

  <WebResourceId> IS A SHARED PLATFORM RESOURCE, NOT UNIQUE TO THIS APP.
  953b9fac-1e5e-e611-80d6-00155ded156f is the id the real export used for a model-driven app
  built through the standard app designer; it is the generic Unified Interface bootstrapper web
  resource every designer-built app of this kind references, not something generated per app,
  so this file uses the same id rather than inventing a different one with no evidence behind it.
-->
<AppModule>
  <UniqueName>rev_grantadministration</UniqueName>
  <IntroducedVersion>1.0.0.0</IntroducedVersion>
  <WebResourceId>953b9fac-1e5e-e611-80d6-00155ded156f</WebResourceId>
  <OptimizedFor></OptimizedFor>
  <statecode>0</statecode>
  <statuscode>1</statuscode>
  <FormFactor>1</FormFactor>
  <ClientType>4</ClientType>
  <NavigationType>0</NavigationType>
  <AppModuleComponents>
    <!-- ── rev_grant ADDED 2026-08-19. THIS LIST IS THE APP, NOT THE SITE MAP ──────────
      rev_grant shipped in WBS 0.4-R with an Entity.xml, a main form, three saved queries and
      a set of privileges, and on 2026-08-19 it got its site-map group and three sub-areas
      too. It still did not render for a user, because a model-driven app renders the tables
      in THIS list and nothing else. The site map only lays out what the app already contains.

      The symptom was precise and misleading: Grants appeared in the app designer's EDIT mode
      — which reads the site map — and was absent in PLAY mode, which reads this list. It
      survived a hard cache refresh, so it looked like a platform propagation problem. It was
      a missing line here.

      So adding a Dataverse table to this app is FOUR changes, not three: the entity, its
      site-map SubArea, this AppModuleComponent, and the audit switch in the environment.
      IMP-0088.
    -->
    <!-- The five tables in the app. -->
    <AppModuleComponent type="1" schemaName="rev_applicant" />
    <AppModuleComponent type="1" schemaName="rev_application" />
    <AppModuleComponent type="1" schemaName="rev_grant" />
    <AppModuleComponent type="1" schemaName="rev_setting" />
    <AppModuleComponent type="1" schemaName="rev_errorlog" />
    <!-- The sitemap that lays them out. -->
    <AppModuleComponent type="62" schemaName="rev_grantadministration" />
  </AppModuleComponents>
  <AppModuleRoleMaps />
  <LocalizedNames>
    <LocalizedName description="REV Grant Administration" languagecode="1033" />
  </LocalizedNames>
</AppModule>
